WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.555
Welcome to another Stories From The
Stores

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.840
I'm here with Ben Russell, curator of mechanical engineering

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.080
and we've got the credo of Joseph Clement, so Ben what's a credo?

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.800
What's a credo, good
question. Well it's a statement of intent

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.860
What this thing does, this little
tiny notebook, it establishes a

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:24.500
moral compass as a young man starting
out in the world.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.920
Okay so question number two, who was Joseph Clement?

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.360
He's a young guy, so when he writes this it was about 1790, it's dated 1793.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.300
So he's about 14 and he eventually becomes one of the

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.080
most incredible engineers in London during the 19th century.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.180
Okay and the big thing he made was the analytical engine?

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.480
So what Clement does is he builds for Babbage the Difference Engine

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.180
which is still in the museum's collection and they are the most astonishing precision machines

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.800
no one else could have done it probably except Clement and those he worked with.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.820
So this is near the Industrial Revolution?

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.559
Yeah so he's up in Cumbria and it's a very
isolated place and he said I'm going to

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:04.200
go to London because that's where it's
all happening at this time

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.672
there's a chance he actually might have taken the book with him.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.040
So he's up there and he is starting to write this sort of stuff, practising his signature

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.440
practicing handwriting, doing little sketches and messing around.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.200
What does he want to do with his life?

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.480
And there's a wonderful little quote which I marked in here

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.660
'the acquisition of knowledge is one
of the most venerable occupations of youth'

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.640
and what he is saying is go
out there and hoover up information and

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.100
you are going to take it in and it will come out in other ways

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:31.400
and he goes out there and he
trains himself up, he's self-taught

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.420
and he ends up as one of the most
significant machine builders in London.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.240
Is it worth someone today writing a
credo?

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.440
I think it probably is. Because it was very clear... what he's doing here is

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.260
setting out the foundations for what he
wants to do next.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.579
Are there any quotes in there that sum up Joseph Clement as a man?

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.700
'We are dazzled with the ostentation, the
pleasure of learning and the noise of victories'

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.140
and actually for all his modesty he was a very victorious man in terms of his career,

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.580
he did some wonderful wonderful things in a subdued sort of way.

